00:06:32.780 --> 00:06:37.350
Seedream gives us room to work with
unusual architecture, scale, and ideas

00:06:37.380 --> 00:06:39.740
that go far beyond everyday locations.

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:43.460
But it can also produce good results with
ordinary places.

00:06:43.460 --> 00:06:46.860
These streets and this forest were created
with Seedream 2.

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:50.880
The lighting is softer, the details are
more understated, and the locations

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:55.590
feel familiar. The lesson PDF will include
guidance on prompting Seedream

00:06:55.760 --> 00:06:57.640
to help you create images like these.

00:06:57.640 --> 00:07:00.220
It's also useful for stylized images.

00:07:00.220 --> 00:07:04.120
Here, the blue tiled room and the red
carpet with scattered records each

00:07:04.120 --> 00:07:06.320
have a strong visual identity.

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:10.460
Color, texture, and lighting give the two
scenes very different moods.

00:07:10.460 --> 00:07:15.390
One thing to watch with Seedream is the
fine line between realism and a CGI

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:19.600
look. In our experience, the more
fantastical the scene and the more details

00:07:19.600 --> 00:07:23.780
we pack into the prompt, the more the
result tends to look like a 3D render.

00:07:23.780 --> 00:07:25.740
You can see that in these environments.

00:07:25.740 --> 00:07:31.060
There's plenty of detail, but the overall
finish feels a little too perfect.

00:07:31.060 --> 00:07:32.700
That can suit some projects.

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:36.320
If you want the image to feel photographed
though, it's a limitation to keep

00:07:36.320 --> 00:07:39.920
in mind. Another weakness is repetition.

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:44.840
Seedream can fill a scene with objects or
textures that look too similar.

00:07:44.840 --> 00:07:47.300
Notice the ferns inside these tanks.

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:50.820
Their shapes and arrangement repeat across
the image.

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:52.860
Something similar happens in this salt
flat.

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:56.680
The raised salt formations have a very
consistent shape and size, creating a

00:07:56.680 --> 00:07:58.720
repeated pattern across much of the
ground.

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:02.600
Now, compare that with Soul Cinema's
result from the same prompt.

00:08:02.600 --> 00:08:07.480
The surface has finer cracks, flatter
areas, and more variation.

00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:11.200
It doesn't have the same prominent
repeating pattern, which makes this

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:13.940
particular result feel more natural.

00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:18.110
Both images describe a salt flat, but the
way each model handles the surface

00:08:18.340 --> 00:08:20.620
gives us a different sense of realism.

00:08:20.620 --> 00:08:23.940
Seedream is also useful for editing
existing images.

00:08:23.940 --> 00:08:27.400
We'll come back to that later in the
lesson and walk through the process in

00:08:27.400 --> 00:08:31.780
detail. For precise text and layouts, we
use GPT Image.

00:08:31.780 --> 00:08:34.820
It's a reliable all-rounder for text and
design.

00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:37.840
One of its biggest strengths is prompt
following.

00:08:37.840 --> 00:08:42.080
It follows detailed instructions very
closely, so you can specify what

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:44.780
should be in the image and where it should
go.

00:08:44.780 --> 00:08:49.310
The more precisely you describe what you
want, the more closely the result

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:51.900
can match your brief. Look at this book.

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:56.120
We've specified the headings, the
paragraph, and the short descriptions on

00:08:56.120 --> 00:08:59.730
the right-hand page. The model placed that
text alongside the illustrations

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:03.240
in a readable layout while keeping the
whole thing looking like an actual

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:06.860
book on a table. It's useful for
storyboards too.

00:09:06.860 --> 00:09:10.200
Here we ask for four shots of one anime
scene.

00:09:10.200 --> 00:09:14.010
That gives us a visual plan for the
sequence before we start generating

00:09:14.020 --> 00:09:17.840
video. And here's a smaller detail, an
American license plate.

00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:21.720
We requested this exact combination of
letters and numbers, along with the

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:24.200
state name and the placement of the
stickers.

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:28.100
This is where precise instructions are
especially useful, when a detail

00:09:28.100 --> 00:09:32.320
needs to say something specific, rather
than just look roughly right.

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:36.280
With locations though, we have found a
different trade-off.

00:09:36.280 --> 00:09:40.040
This model can make the image feel
overloaded with detail.

00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:44.530
Textures can look over-sharpened, and too
much of the scene competes for

00:09:44.620 --> 00:09:46.980
attention, reducing the sense of depth.

00:09:46.980 --> 00:09:49.480
Look closely at the sky above this road.

00:09:49.480 --> 00:09:53.530
There's a visible noise texture across an
area that should feel much

00:09:53.620 --> 00:09:57.980
smoother. We've seen this kind of noise
spread across GPT-generated images,

00:09:57.980 --> 00:10:00.400
rather than staying within individual
materials.

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:04.020
In the lantern-lit street, notice the
sharply defined surfaces and the

00:10:04.020 --> 00:10:07.800
polished finish. There's foreground blur,
but parts of the environment still

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:11.240
feel closer to a CGI render than a
photograph location.

00:10:11.240 --> 00:10:16.040
For text, storyboards, and details that
need to follow a precise brief, GPT

00:10:16.040 --> 00:10:17.620
is one of our strongest options.

00:10:17.620 --> 00:10:22.150
For locations, we also need to judge the
depth, texture, and overall

00:10:22.260 --> 00:10:25.820
photographic feel. And then there's Nano
Banana Pro.

00:10:25.820 --> 00:10:30.610
Like GPT, it follows prompts closely, and
can handle a wide range of image

00:10:30.620 --> 00:10:35.160
tasks. The trade-off with both GPT and
Nano is that their results tend to

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:39.850
have a more obvious AI look than Soul's,
that default

00:10:40.300 --> 00:10:44.350
artificial finish. So, look at the style
as well as how closely the image

00:10:44.420 --> 00:10:49.600
follows your request. You can use any
model

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:54.760
you like. We mainly use Soul and Seedream
5.0 Pro for the

00:10:54.760 --> 00:10:59.590
best cinematic results. So, why are we
spending all this time on still

00:10:59.620 --> 00:11:04.480
images in a video course? Think of it like
choosing your camera,

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:07.440
lighting, and visual style before a shoot.

00:11:07.440 --> 00:11:11.970
By the time you generate the video, many
of those decisions are already

00:11:12.020 --> 00:11:13.300
embedded in the reference.

00:11:13.300 --> 00:11:15.340
Look at these two opening clips.

00:11:15.340 --> 00:11:19.500
Same prompt, different assets, and a
completely different feel.

00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:23.100
If you're aiming for live action, but your
reference has plastic textures,

00:11:23.100 --> 00:11:27.090
strange edges, or an artificial finish,
the video can carry those features

00:11:27.100 --> 00:11:31.100
forward. That's why it's so important to
spend time preparing your assets.

00:11:31.100 --> 00:11:34.540
High-quality images are a major part of
what it takes to create a video that

00:11:34.540 --> 00:11:38.500
stands out. Before you start generation,
take a closer look.

00:11:38.500 --> 00:11:40.880
Do the materials feel believable?

00:11:40.880 --> 00:11:42.680
Does the light make sense?

00:11:42.680 --> 00:11:44.820
Does the mood fit your scene?

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:48.490
That's where a professional workflow
starts, making these visual decisions

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:53.370
deliberately. You choose the face, the
place, the textures, and the mood

00:11:53.500 --> 00:11:55.860
because they serve your scene.

00:11:55.860 --> 00:12:00.730
Get those choices right and you give the
video a much stronger starting

00:12:00.740 --> 00:12:03.560
point. One secret technique before we move
on.

00:12:03.560 --> 00:12:08.140
Add out-of-focus elements in the
foreground to create more depth and

00:12:08.140 --> 00:12:12.000
simulate the look of a wide open aperture
in your generated video.

00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:15.550
You can also include shot on an anamorphic
lens in your Soul Cinema prompt

00:12:15.660 --> 00:12:19.900
to create the distinctive blur and bokeh
associated with anamorphic glass.

00:12:19.900 --> 00:12:23.770
Carrying that look into your video can
make the final result feel more

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:26.540
cinematic and visually distinctive.

