00:15:10.740 --> 00:15:14.840
identity cues and the character's face may
change between wide shots and

00:15:14.840 --> 00:15:19.850
close ups. I want to add and change a few
details to make this character's

00:15:19.920 --> 00:15:21.660
identity more distinctive.

00:15:21.660 --> 00:15:24.460
Now, let's make a few small changes to his
outfit.

00:15:24.460 --> 00:15:27.440
I'll use Sea Dream 5.0 Pro for this.

00:15:27.440 --> 00:15:31.020
It lets us adjust individual details while
keeping the character and the

00:15:31.020 --> 00:15:36.250
image's textures intact. Let's add a
wallet chain and change the t-shirt

00:15:36.460 --> 00:15:39.310
while keeping everything else as it is.

00:15:39.440 --> 00:15:43.950
I'll drop our character sheet into the
input, then I'll click in the prompt

00:15:43.980 --> 00:15:45.780
box and describe those changes.

00:15:45.780 --> 00:15:49.490
Give him a silver wallet chain hanging off
his jeans pocket.

00:15:49.520 --> 00:15:53.900
Replace the black t-shirt with a plain
white ribbed cotton tank top.

00:15:53.900 --> 00:15:55.640
Same tank top in all views.

00:15:55.640 --> 00:16:00.220
I specify the same tank top in every view
so the sheet stays consistent.

00:16:00.220 --> 00:16:04.260
Then I click generate. Let's put it next
to the original.

00:16:04.260 --> 00:16:07.480
The face, jacket, and textures have stayed
the same.

00:16:07.480 --> 00:16:09.900
We've just added the chain and changed the
shirt.

00:16:09.900 --> 00:16:14.500
I'll like this version, then download the
finished character sheet.

00:16:14.500 --> 00:16:19.200
That keeps my chosen result easy to find
and ready to use as a reference for

00:16:19.200 --> 00:16:22.980
the next shots. For this scene, I'll need
one more character, this time with

00:16:22.980 --> 00:16:25.440
my own face but still in biker clothing.

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:27.840
I'll stay in the same AI director chat and
type.

00:16:27.840 --> 00:16:32.290
I need one more character sheet using the
face from the first image and the

00:16:32.320 --> 00:16:33.860
outfit from the second image.

00:16:33.860 --> 00:16:37.970
I drag in my photo first, then an image of
one of the bikers we generated

00:16:38.100 --> 00:16:41.790
earlier. I want to use his outfit for this
character.

00:16:41.860 --> 00:16:46.410
Then I send the request. The director
asks, "The second image cuts off

00:16:46.520 --> 00:16:50.380
before the shoes. How should I complete
the lower outfit?" I'll choose the

00:16:50.380 --> 00:16:53.460
recommended option gray jeans and ankle
boots.

00:16:53.460 --> 00:16:58.400
The description specifies full length
straight jeans resting over the boots.

00:16:58.400 --> 00:17:01.460
That's the look I want, so I select it and
submit.

00:17:01.460 --> 00:17:04.800
With those details confirmed it prepares
another character sheet.

00:17:04.800 --> 00:17:06.900
I review the prompt and click generate.

00:17:06.900 --> 00:17:08.060
The first version is ready.

00:17:08.060 --> 00:17:11.360
Let's check the outfit. The director
spotted something that doesn't match

00:17:11.360 --> 00:17:15.740
our request. It says the face and jacket
match but the jeans are tucked into

00:17:15.740 --> 00:17:19.100
taller boots and asks whether it should
correct them before finishing.

00:17:19.100 --> 00:17:23.960
You can see it here. The boots are too
high and the jeans are tucked inside.

00:17:23.960 --> 00:17:26.780
I wanted the fabric to fall over ankle
height boots.

00:17:26.780 --> 00:17:29.460
I go back and choose correct the jeans and
boots.

00:17:29.460 --> 00:17:32.620
The face, jacket and layout should stay
the same.

00:17:32.620 --> 00:17:34.900
Only the lower outfit needs another pass.

00:17:34.900 --> 00:17:36.560
The director prepares the correction.

00:17:36.560 --> 00:17:39.220
I check the prompt, then click generate
again.

00:17:39.220 --> 00:17:43.380
There we go. The jeans now fall over the
boots just as we asked.

00:17:43.380 --> 00:17:46.800
It also prepares the version with the
smaller face removed just as it did

00:17:46.800 --> 00:17:50.930
for our first character. Now I have my own
character in the biker outfit

00:17:51.120 --> 00:17:55.530
ready for the scene. We use AI director to
make character creation faster

00:17:55.580 --> 00:18:00.020
and easier. But let's take a quick look
under the hood to understand what

00:18:00.020 --> 00:18:04.620
makes a character feel unique and how to
assemble your dream cast.

00:18:04.620 --> 00:18:09.420
At the heart of the character sheet skill
is Soul 2.0, a model we haven't

00:18:09.420 --> 00:18:14.300
covered yet. It is designed to create
distinctive, realistic characters and

00:18:14.300 --> 00:18:17.610
clothing. To show you what I mean by
distinctive, let's run a quick

00:18:17.800 --> 00:18:22.920
experiment using the same simple prompt in
three models, Soul 2.0, Seed

00:18:22.920 --> 00:18:27.440
Dream and Nano Banana. For this test, I'll
create a separate folder by

00:18:27.440 --> 00:18:32.270
clicking the plus icon next to the
character folder and naming it model

00:18:32.380 --> 00:18:36.990
testing. Inside the new folder, I'll
select Soul 2.0 and enter this

00:18:37.020 --> 00:18:40.380
prompt. Portrait of a man in his mid
thirties.

00:18:40.380 --> 00:18:45.150
I'll run six generations, then switch to
the other two models and generate

00:18:45.280 --> 00:18:48.180
six images with each of them as well.

00:18:48.180 --> 00:18:49.880
All three batches are ready.

00:18:49.880 --> 00:18:53.200
With Soul, we get six different faces.

00:18:53.200 --> 00:18:55.220
Any of them could work for our scene.

00:18:55.220 --> 00:18:59.520
Seedream and Nano Banana, on the other
hand, produced six very similar

00:18:59.520 --> 00:19:04.060
looking people. Nano Banana even gave us
what could pass for twins.

00:19:04.060 --> 00:19:07.560
These models tend to create an averaged,
idealized appearance.

00:19:07.560 --> 00:19:10.960
They reduce variation and avoid
imperfections in their results.

00:19:10.960 --> 00:19:15.350
You can refine the prompt and describe a
face in great detail, but even then

00:19:15.360 --> 00:19:18.620
it can be difficult to guarantee a truly
unique character.

00:19:18.620 --> 00:19:20.820
Take a closer look at Soul's face.

00:19:20.820 --> 00:19:23.560
They feel like photographs of real people.

00:19:23.560 --> 00:19:28.050
Imperfect skin, unusual features, and
those little details that give a face

00:19:28.220 --> 00:19:31.580
personality. There is also a significant
difference in cost.

00:19:31.580 --> 00:19:36.200
A single 2K generation in Nano Banana
costs three credits, while a generation

00:19:36.200 --> 00:19:40.740
at the same resolution in Soul costs just
0.125 credits.

00:19:40.740 --> 00:19:44.770
With short prompts like this, you can
quickly cast characters for your ideas

00:19:44.940 --> 00:19:48.790
at a very low cost. Let's move on and
learn how to create a

00:19:48.840 --> 00:19:51.110
location.

00:19:52.460 --> 00:19:56.260
While creating the character, I was
already thinking about where I wanted to

00:19:56.260 --> 00:20:01.270
place him. A small bar, somewhere on the
outskirts of town, busy with

00:20:01.320 --> 00:20:04.820
people, with a bar counter, and a pool
table.

00:20:04.820 --> 00:20:08.090
To keep the project organized, I'll create
a separate folder called

00:20:08.140 --> 00:20:12.790
location. I go straight to the prompt box
and make sure Soul Cinema is

00:20:12.800 --> 00:20:17.950
selected. I set the aspect ratio to 21 by
9, which gives the

00:20:18.080 --> 00:20:22.960
composition more room and establishes a
cinematic widescreen look from the

00:20:22.960 --> 00:20:27.110
start. I could simply type American bar,
and that would already produce

00:20:27.160 --> 00:20:31.000
interesting results. But I have a clear
picture of how I want the space to

00:20:31.000 --> 00:20:33.640
look, so I use a more specific prompt.

00:20:33.640 --> 00:20:36.280
Create an empty American dive bar.

00:20:36.280 --> 00:20:40.430
Place a dark wooden bar counter on the
right, with leather stools and

00:20:40.440 --> 00:20:42.160
illuminated bottles behind it.

00:20:42.160 --> 00:20:47.060
Add a few neon signs, booths along the
left wall, and a pool table at the

00:20:47.060 --> 00:20:50.020
far end beneath a hanging lamp.

00:20:50.020 --> 00:20:52.060
Frame the shot from the doorway.

00:20:52.060 --> 00:20:54.820
Now I'll run four generations and compare
the results.

00:20:54.820 --> 00:20:58.900
Four versions are ready. Let's see which
one gets closest to the bar I had

00:20:58.900 --> 00:21:03.320
in mind. The first one is promising, but
the heavy grain makes it feel too

00:21:03.320 --> 00:21:08.480
much like a 1990s film. The second feels
too empty and lacks the

00:21:08.480 --> 00:21:12.880
detail I'm looking for. The third is
closest to what I pictured, so I won't

00:21:12.880 --> 00:21:17.910
change the prompt. I'll use this image as
the base for the next step.

00:21:20.040 --> 00:21:23.280
So we have our bar. Let's adapt it to our
scene.

00:21:23.280 --> 00:21:27.760
A first result usually needs a few tweaks,
sometimes a bigger edit.

00:21:27.760 --> 00:21:29.160
That's just part of the process.

00:21:29.160 --> 00:21:34.070
In this case, I want to fill the bar with
regulars and add one more

00:21:34.320 --> 00:21:37.840
important detail that will help me when I
generate the video.

00:21:37.840 --> 00:21:42.000
I've already created a separate folder
where we'll work on the location.

00:21:42.000 --> 00:21:47.040
As I mentioned earlier in the lesson, we
usually use C Dream 5.0 Pro for

00:21:47.040 --> 00:21:51.830
editing. I drag the downloaded location
image into the prompt box and

00:21:51.860 --> 00:21:54.000
describe what I want to change.

00:21:54.000 --> 00:21:57.780
For straightforward edits like these,
plain language is all you need.

00:21:57.780 --> 00:22:02.400
Add eight dive bar regulars, a mix of men
and women.

00:22:02.400 --> 00:22:05.960
Some are playing pool, others are sitting
at tables.

00:22:05.960 --> 00:22:10.460
Keep all bar stools empty and place a
single mug of beer in the middle of

00:22:10.460 --> 00:22:15.170
the bar counter. I'll keep the original
aspect ratio and generate four

00:22:15.340 --> 00:22:17.680
variations. There we go. They're ready.

00:22:17.680 --> 00:22:20.160
Let's check the changes. This one works.

00:22:20.160 --> 00:22:24.200
We've got people in the bar, the stools
are empty, and the beer mug is right

00:22:24.200 --> 00:22:25.340
in the middle of the counter.

00:22:25.340 --> 00:22:30.520
The mug acts as a spatial anchor, an
object I can reference when describing

00:22:30.520 --> 00:22:33.080
the action in my video prompt.

00:22:33.080 --> 00:22:37.460
Instead of explaining the entire layout, I
can simply say my character is

00:22:37.460 --> 00:22:41.780
sitting on the bar stool beside the beer
mug in the middle of the counter.

00:22:41.780 --> 00:22:45.860
That gives sedents enough context to place
him correctly.

00:22:45.860 --> 00:22:49.360
Adding clear visual anchors to a location
is a useful habit.

00:22:49.360 --> 00:22:53.720
It saves you time, makes spatial
instructions less ambiguous, and increases

00:22:53.720 --> 00:22:58.030
your chances of getting a successful video
generation.

00:22:59.940 --> 00:23:03.620
For my scene, I need one more view of this
location.

00:23:03.620 --> 00:23:07.980
A reverse angle facing the door my
character will walk through.

00:23:07.980 --> 00:23:11.680
I start a new chat in AI director and
describe the shot.

00:23:11.680 --> 00:23:16.850
I ask it to use SeaDream 5.0 with the
camera inside the bar

00:23:17.140 --> 00:23:18.480
looking toward the doorway.

00:23:18.480 --> 00:23:22.880
I want a tighter view close to the
entrance with the door and just a couple

00:23:22.880 --> 00:23:26.500
of stools in frame. Then I drag in the bar
image as a reference and send the

00:23:26.500 --> 00:23:29.720
request. The director uses that image and
my instructions to work out the

00:23:29.720 --> 00:23:33.680
new angle. Alright, that one's finished,
let's have a look.

00:23:33.680 --> 00:23:37.290
We're now looking towards the entrance
with the counter and stools in the

00:23:37.320 --> 00:23:40.920
foreground, and it even created a jig box
beside the door.

00:23:40.920 --> 00:23:43.560
This gives me the view I need for my
character's arrival.

00:23:43.560 --> 00:23:47.750
We now have all the location assets we
need, and we're ready to move

00:23:47.800 --> 00:23:53.170
on. The next step in preparing the scene

00:23:53.300 --> 00:23:57.800
is creating the props. You can use any of
the models we covered earlier for

00:23:57.800 --> 00:24:02.930
this. To show you how their results
differ, I'll compare four models using

00:24:03.060 --> 00:24:07.260
exactly the same prompt. For my scene, I
need a briefcase.

00:24:07.260 --> 00:24:11.400
I want it to stand out both in my
character's hand and against the location,

00:24:11.400 --> 00:24:13.780
so I'll make it bright orange.

00:24:13.780 --> 00:24:18.190
This creates a clear focal point in the
frame and makes the audience wonder

00:24:18.560 --> 00:24:20.200
what might be hidden inside.

00:24:20.200 --> 00:24:24.280
I use this prompt, and here's what each of
the four image models produced.

00:24:24.280 --> 00:24:28.290
A slim orange hard shell briefcase with a
ribbed molded lid, rounded

00:24:28.420 --> 00:24:31.420
corners, a black top handle, and two metal
latches.

00:24:31.420 --> 00:24:34.920
White background, soft studio light, no
logos or text.

00:24:34.920 --> 00:24:37.780
For this scene, I prefer the first
briefcase.

00:24:37.780 --> 00:24:42.190
I like its slightly porous texture, and
the three quarter angle gives

00:24:42.320 --> 00:24:45.840
c-dance clear information about both the
front and the side.

00:24:45.840 --> 00:24:50.320
That leaves less room for the model to
interpret its shape, and helps the

00:24:50.320 --> 00:24:52.860
prop remain consistent from shot to shot.

00:24:52.860 --> 00:24:57.140
For more complex objects, with different
details on the front, back, and

00:24:57.140 --> 00:25:01.140
sides, it's better to create a prop sheet
that shows every important angle

00:25:01.140 --> 00:25:05.500
and feature. For this briefcase, a prop
sheet could look like this.

00:25:05.500 --> 00:25:09.370
We'll cover how to prepare detailed assets
like this, along with more

00:25:09.540 --> 00:25:13.700
advanced techniques for creating and
editing characters in our advanced

00:25:13.700 --> 00:25:17.540
course. Now that all the assets are
prepared and organized, I'm ready to

00:25:17.540 --> 00:25:22.480
move on to video generation, a process
with even more variables to

00:25:22.480 --> 00:25:27.970
consider. But first, it's time for your
homework.

00:25:29.300 --> 00:25:33.240
Now it's your turn to prepare assets for a
scene of your own.

00:25:33.240 --> 00:25:37.630
Create one or two characters, and a
character sheet for each.

00:25:37.660 --> 00:25:41.060
Then, generate a location where your scene
will take place.

00:25:41.060 --> 00:25:44.080
Make a few deliberate edits to that
location.

00:25:44.080 --> 00:25:49.370
You could add people, change an object, or
introduce a detail your

00:25:49.380 --> 00:25:52.820
character will interact with.

00:25:52.820 --> 00:25:56.520
Think about what needs to happen in the
scene, and make your edits serve

00:25:56.520 --> 00:26:01.110
that idea. Upload your character sheets
and the before and after location

00:26:01.200 --> 00:26:05.690
images to the platform. Include a short
description of your scene, and

00:26:05.740 --> 00:26:08.570
explain what you changed.

00:26:10.380 --> 00:26:14.680
Now, you should have your cast and your
location, but how do you bring them

00:26:14.680 --> 00:26:18.800
together and turn separate images into a
scene that feels connected?

00:26:18.800 --> 00:26:22.720
In lesson five, we'll place our characters
into the environment and start

00:26:22.720 --> 00:26:24.340
building the scene in motion.

00:26:24.340 --> 00:26:29.420
We'll take a closer look at CDEN's 2.5,
how it uses your references, how to

00:26:29.420 --> 00:26:33.140
guide the action, and how to get the
characters and camera to do what you

00:26:33.140 --> 00:26:37.320
have in mind. So, bring the assets you've
prepared and we'll put them to

00:26:37.320 --> 00:26:40.070
work in the next lesson. See you there.
